projects.description:
The Green, digital and inclusive private sector development (GDI PSD) project is a comprehensive programme financed by the European Union (EU) that seeks to develop a greener, digitalised and more inclusive private sector in Ghana (including in the creative and heritage, healthcare and pharmaceutical industries). This will be achieved through three major outputs:  
i.	improved access to innovative and inclusive finance for digital, green and circular initiatives;  
ii.	improved and inclusive business regulatory environment and ecosystem for digital, green and circular initiatives;  
iii.	enhanced capacity of entrepreneurs to apply green and digital technologies.  

The target of this project is represented by small and growing companies (SGCs), who are the which largely consist of Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises (SMEs), in most part. An estimated 92 percent of Ghanaian businesses are micro, small or medium-sized enterprises. They employ 80 percent of the workforce and generate 70 percent of the country’s GDP, and nearly half (46 percent) of these are owned by women. Proportionally SGCs are employing the larger share of the workforce. SGCs are defined as “commercially viable businesses with five to 250 employees that have significant potential, and ambition, for growth”.

